  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nPatchy fog has developed early this morning as temperatures drop \ninto the 40s across the region. Expect it to become dense in \nsome locations, but improve quickly after sunrise. Today will be\nanother beautiful fall day with mild temperatures and mostly \nsunny skies. Clouds will increase overnight and an area of \nshowers may move into western Minnesota during the afternoon as \na pocket of higher vorticity values spreads northeast from the \nSioux Falls area. Those showers could continue further east as \nthe latest HRRR and RRFS depict, though it is more likely that a\ndry boundary layer will limit the amount of rain that makes it \nto the surface.\n\nIt is not until Saturday that a weakening cold front attempts to \nsweep through the Upper Midwest. Increased moisture from the \nPacific is expected to support multiple rounds of showers as the\nfront stalls out and interacts with several upper level \nshortwaves heading into next week. WPC's QPF has markedly \nincreased during the Monday timeframe, generating a strip of 0.5\nto 1.0 inches from southwest towards east-central MN. While \nthat location may shift, we can confidently say that the pattern\nwill be changed heading into next week, especially when looking\nat temperatures. Highs will likely dip just below normal for \nMonday and Tuesday, before recovering slightly by the middle of \nthe week.",
